.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OSSL_CMP_VALIDATE_MSG 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm OSSL_CMP_validate_msg ,
.Nm OSSL_CMP_validate_cert_path
.Nd functions for verifying CMP message protection
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/cmp.h
.Ft int
.Fo OSSL_CMP_validate_msg
.Fa "OSSL_CMP_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "OSSL_CMP_MSG *msg"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo OSSL_CMP_validate_cert_path
.Fa "const OSSL_CMP_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "X509_STORE *trusted_store"
.Fa "X509 *cert"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This is the API for validating the protection of CMP messages, which
includes validating CMP message sender certificates and their paths
while optionally checking the revocation status of the certificates(s).
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_validate_msg
validates the protection of the given
.Fa msg ,
which must be signature-based or using password-based MAC (PBM). In the
former case a suitable trust anchor must be given in the CMP context
.Fa ctx ,
and in the latter case the matching secret must have been set there
using
.Xr OSSL_CMP_CTX_set1_secretValue 3 .
.Pp
In case of signature algorithm, the certificate to use for the signature
check is preferably the one provided by a call to
.Xr OSSL_CMP_CTX_set1_srvCert 3 .
If no such sender cert has been pinned then candidate sender
certificates are taken from the list of certificates received in the
.Fa msg
extraCerts, then any certificates provided before via
.Xr OSSL_CMP_CTX_set1_untrusted 3 ,
and then all trusted certificates provided via
.Xr OSSL_CMP_CTX_set0_trusted 3 .
A candidate certificate is acceptable only if it is currently valid (or
the trust store contains a verification callback that overrides the
verdict that the certificate is expired or not yet valid), its subject
DN matches the
.Fa msg
sender DN (as far as present), and its subject key identifier is present
and matches the senderKID (as far as the latter is present).
Each acceptable cert is tried in the given order to see if the message
signature check succeeds and the cert and its path can be verified using
any trust store set via
.Xr OSSL_CMP_CTX_set0_trusted 3 .
.Pp
If the option OSSL_CMP_OPT_PERMIT_TA_IN_EXTRACERTS_FOR_IR was set by
calling
.Xr OSSL_CMP_CTX_set_option 3 ,
for an Initialization Response (IP) message any self-issued certificate
from the
.Fa msg
extraCerts field may be used as a trust anchor for the path verification
of an 'acceptable' cert if it can be used also to validate the issued
certificate returned in the IP message.
This is according to TS 33.310 [Network Domain Security (NDS);
Authentication Framework (AF)] document specified by The 3rd Generation
Partnership Project (3GPP). Note that using this option is dangerous as
the certificate obtained this way has not been authenticated (at least
not at CMP level).
Taking it over as a trust anchor implements trust-on-first-use (TOFU).
.Pp
Any cert that has been found as described above is cached and tried
first when validating the signatures of subsequent messages in the same
transaction.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_validate_cert_path
attempts to validate the given certificate and its path using the given
store of trusted certs (possibly including CRLs and a cert verification
callback) and non-trusted intermediate certs from the
.Fa ctx .
.Sh NOTES
CMP is defined in RFC 4210 (and CRMF in RFC 4211).
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OSSL_CMP_validate_msg
and
.Fn OSSL_CMP_validate_cert_path
return 1 on success, 0 on error or validation failed.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr OSSL_CMP_CTX_new 3 ,
.Xr OSSL_CMP_exec_certreq 3 ,
.Xr OSSL_CMP_CTX_set1_secretValue 3 ,
.Xr OSSL_CMP_CTX_set1_srvCert 3 ,
.Xr OSSL_CMP_CTX_set1_untrusted 3 ,
.Xr OSSL_CMP_CTX_set0_trusted 3
.Sh HISTORY
The OpenSSL CMP support was added in OpenSSL 3.0.
